1
00:00:03,038 --> 00:00:09,684
[SPEAKER_04]: So, this is some excerpts from a talk by Ajahn Chah called Right View, the Place of Coolness.

2
00:00:10,705 --> 00:00:11,185
Coolness.

3
00:00:12,927 --> 00:00:14,648
[SPEAKER_04]: Just a few paragraphs.

4
00:00:18,912 --> 00:00:21,514
[SPEAKER_04]: The practice of dhamma goes against our habits.

5
00:00:22,315 --> 00:00:24,136
[SPEAKER_04]: The truth goes against our desires.

6
00:00:25,097 --> 00:00:27,239
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there is difficulty in the practice.

7
00:00:28,576 --> 00:00:34,620
[SPEAKER_04]: Some things which we understand as wrong, may be right, while the things we take to be right, may be wrong.

8
00:00:35,881 --> 00:00:36,442
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is this?

9
00:00:37,222 --> 00:00:39,064
[SPEAKER_04]: Because our minds are in darkness.

10
00:00:39,544 --> 00:00:41,205
[SPEAKER_04]: We don't clearly see the truth.

11
00:00:42,246 --> 00:00:45,609
[SPEAKER_04]: We don't really know anything, and so are fooled by people's lies.

12
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:50,032
[SPEAKER_04]: They point out what is right as being wrong, and we believe it.

13
00:00:50,953 --> 00:00:54,155
[SPEAKER_04]: That which is wrong, they say is right, and we believe that.

14
00:00:55,148 --> 00:00:57,610
[SPEAKER_04]: This is because we are not yet our own masters.

15
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:00,752
[SPEAKER_04]: Our moods lie to us constantly.

16
00:01:01,713 --> 00:01:07,197
[SPEAKER_04]: We shouldn't take this mind and its opinions as our guide, because it doesn't know the truth.

17
00:01:12,621 --> 00:01:13,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that, that's the paragraph done?

18
00:01:14,422 --> 00:01:14,582
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

19
00:01:14,842 --> 00:01:21,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, for example, that's a very good example of how differently you can take what he just said.

20
00:01:21,962 --> 00:01:30,809
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, obviously, you can't trust your moods, you don't have a criteria, you don't have a right reference point to know whether you are right and what's right is right and wrong is wrong, as the Buddha would say.

21
00:01:31,729 --> 00:01:43,318
[SPEAKER_00]: But most people that I heard they would be palloting Ajahn Chah, would use this very thing he would say to justify dismissing everything and not committing to anything, because everything is not sure.

22
00:01:44,727 --> 00:01:46,088
[SPEAKER_00]: But he doesn't say that, clearly.

23
00:01:46,648 --> 00:01:48,390
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, yeah, that's your starting point.

24
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:53,253
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't trust things until you become your master and you know right is right and wrong is wrong.

25
00:01:53,954 --> 00:01:57,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, those same people would be saying, oh, nothing is sure.

26
00:01:57,156 --> 00:01:58,097
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't trust anything.

27
00:01:58,117 --> 00:01:58,877
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just opinion.

28
00:01:58,897 --> 00:01:59,718
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just a view.

29
00:02:00,198 --> 00:02:03,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, at the same time, then develop a view that there is nothing to be attained as well.

30
00:02:03,541 --> 00:02:07,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, of course, when you're dismissing everything, there is no goal for you.

31
00:02:07,664 --> 00:02:10,846
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no place that you can sort of achieve through your efforts.

32
00:02:11,287 --> 00:02:13,688
[SPEAKER_00]: They would then go beyond the views and you would be the master of

33
00:02:15,358 --> 00:02:16,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the suttas, that's all they talk about.

34
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:20,960
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see Ajahn Chah, that's all he talks about.

35
00:02:22,781 --> 00:02:24,441
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I like that last sentence.

36
00:02:24,481 --> 00:02:27,702
[SPEAKER_04]: We shouldn't take this mind and its opinions as our guide.

37
00:02:27,722 --> 00:02:27,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

38
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:30,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say that, aha, okay.

39
00:02:30,143 --> 00:02:32,904
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I must not hold to any views.

40
00:02:34,224 --> 00:02:36,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that that is the view you hold on to.

41
00:02:37,726 --> 00:02:39,446
[SPEAKER_00]: View of I will not commit to any view.

42
00:02:40,367 --> 00:02:44,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you should not commit to any view, so that you can keep looking for the right view.

43
00:02:45,291 --> 00:02:48,593
[SPEAKER_00]: But, not like, I will not commit to any view as a goal in itself.

44
00:02:50,314 --> 00:02:55,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember even the chant that's done in the temples in Europe, uh, I will not hold to fixed views.

45
00:02:55,997 --> 00:02:57,437
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a fixed view in itself.

46
00:02:57,758 --> 00:02:59,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Refusing to hold to fixed views.

47
00:03:01,039 --> 00:03:05,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And even the Buddha said that, the same, when the man said, no view pleases me.

48
00:03:06,562 --> 00:03:09,924
[SPEAKER_00]: And he, and the Buddha told him, well, surely that view is pleasing to you.

49
00:03:10,364 --> 00:03:12,045
[SPEAKER_00]: The one that everything is displeasing to me.

50
00:03:12,632 --> 00:03:17,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people think they can just find a way out of views through not committing to any views, and that's a quite a popular belief.

51
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:24,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, they would quote people like Ajahn Chah saying... and you see how much misquoting there would be, because he doesn't say that.

52
00:03:25,864 --> 00:03:28,627
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, your starting point is you don't know right is right, wrong is wrong.

53
00:03:28,687 --> 00:03:30,108
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a pretentious place.

54
00:03:30,568 --> 00:03:35,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to make the effort to clarify, not make the effort and not commit to anything, because everything is risky and dangerous.

55
00:03:45,032 --> 00:03:47,394
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I'll continue.

56
00:03:48,215 --> 00:03:54,921
[SPEAKER_04]: Some people don't want to listen to others at all, but this is not the way of a man, a wise man.

57
00:03:55,881 --> 00:03:56,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, okay.

58
00:03:56,302 --> 00:03:57,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, let me add to that as well.

59
00:03:57,583 --> 00:04:01,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, people might think, oh, no, I have no problem listening to others.

60
00:04:02,567 --> 00:04:04,028
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you really want to take that seriously.

61
00:04:04,609 --> 00:04:11,195
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when, when a person, like you ask yourself, if I were to face someone else's view,

62
00:04:11,947 --> 00:04:16,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, say, I think this... what practice is, I think it's like this, and you do that, and whatever.

63
00:04:16,648 --> 00:04:23,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, somebody else comes and challenges that, or not even personally challenges me, but simply has a different view.

64
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:27,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to ask yourself, would I be affected by that?

65
00:04:29,551 --> 00:04:30,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I threatened?

66
00:04:30,732 --> 00:04:36,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I feel I need to defend myself on account of opposing views that don't agree with me?

67
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:40,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I feel I need to justify myself to myself?

68
00:04:41,006 --> 00:04:46,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, I might not get into an argument with that person, but am I personally affected by the opposing view?

69
00:04:47,327 --> 00:04:48,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it cause the doubt?

70
00:04:48,167 --> 00:04:49,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it make unrest?

71
00:04:50,308 --> 00:04:52,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Me experience unrest or similar?

72
00:04:52,828 --> 00:04:54,889
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, that's what it means.

73
00:04:55,609 --> 00:04:56,589
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to hear others.

74
00:04:57,469 --> 00:05:00,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you're not wise, and that's why you're not developing wisdom.

75
00:05:00,370 --> 00:05:03,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you don't go and... oh, I'll go and debate with everybody that... No.

76
00:05:03,431 --> 00:05:04,351
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't learn like that.

77
00:05:04,411 --> 00:05:06,851
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn by understanding right is right, wrong is wrong.

78
00:05:07,791 --> 00:05:09,952
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you keep protecting whatever you got,

79
00:05:10,938 --> 00:05:17,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of fear of maybe you're wrong, means you won't know wrong is wrong and right is right, because you're not committing to it.

80
00:05:20,707 --> 00:05:29,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's absolutely no reason to be affected by anything, let alone someone else's view, to cause emotion arise in you or anything like that.

81
00:05:29,735 --> 00:05:35,420
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no justification for that.

82
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:37,422
[SPEAKER_04]: A wise man listens to everything.

83
00:05:38,972 --> 00:05:40,212
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what does that mean?

84
00:05:40,272 --> 00:05:42,073
[SPEAKER_04]: He listens and contemplates.

85
00:06:03,052 --> 00:06:06,334
[SPEAKER_00]: He listens and he understands what that thing is saying.

86
00:06:06,594 --> 00:06:07,894
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands the meaning of it.

87
00:06:08,555 --> 00:06:12,536
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how he can know whether it's right or not, whether it's according to the dhamma or not.

88
00:06:14,898 --> 00:06:19,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you understand the meaning of something, if you never make the effort to understand it?

89
00:06:21,761 --> 00:06:28,924
[SPEAKER_00]: If your efforts are about not committing to anything, not holding to any views, watching your nostrils, how can you then understand that?

90
00:06:29,004 --> 00:06:29,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

91
00:06:30,005 --> 00:06:30,765
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you don't.

92
00:06:34,938 --> 00:06:39,742
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if the teacher speaks the truth, don't just believe it, because you don't yet know the truth.

93
00:06:39,762 --> 00:06:40,762
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to understand it.

94
00:06:41,043 --> 00:06:45,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And not, oh, you must understand it for yourself, as in, are you going to have a mystical experience for yourself?

95
00:06:45,906 --> 00:06:47,027
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you need to understand it.

96
00:06:47,427 --> 00:06:49,409
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you understand it, you will be able to explain it.

97
00:06:50,650 --> 00:06:53,292
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be able to put it in words.

98
00:06:53,312 --> 00:06:58,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Not made it completely inexpressible, idiosyncratic experience that nobody can relate to it, but you know it's my truth.

99
00:06:59,416 --> 00:07:00,997
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's just a mystical experience.

100
00:07:01,137 --> 00:07:01,958
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the truth of it.

101
00:07:03,462 --> 00:07:12,606
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to understand the universal nature of experience that's in common to all beings that contain five... that are made of five aggregates, which is all beings.

102
00:07:12,626 --> 00:07:13,546
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you'll be free.

103
00:07:14,407 --> 00:07:15,807
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why anyone can relate to it.

104
00:07:15,847 --> 00:07:16,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

105
00:07:17,388 --> 00:07:20,229
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the dhamma is the path that doesn't change.

106
00:07:20,269 --> 00:07:22,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's revealed or not, it stays the same.

107
00:07:22,110 --> 00:07:23,771
[SPEAKER_00]: The universal principles remain the same.

108
00:07:29,044 --> 00:07:33,846
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can't be revealed to you if you if you're not making effort to reveal it, to discern it.

109
00:07:36,128 --> 00:07:40,090
[SPEAKER_00]: It just cannot be given, cannot occur accidentally or spontaneously.

110
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:42,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Suddenly you spontaneously develop wisdom.

111
00:07:43,571 --> 00:07:47,253
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's quite common to say.

112
00:07:48,014 --> 00:07:50,135
[SPEAKER_04]: I feel, I know that... Yeah, exactly.

113
00:07:50,355 --> 00:07:51,455
[SPEAKER_04]: I know that I know.

114
00:07:51,595 --> 00:07:54,177
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't put it in words, but it was a real thing.

115
00:07:54,397 --> 00:07:55,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it was a real thing as such.

116
00:07:57,418 --> 00:07:58,759
[SPEAKER_00]: A real mystical experience.

117
00:07:59,419 --> 00:08:01,840
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can't put it in words, you haven't understood it.

118
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:08,142
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say, as far as the language, as far as the designation goes, the Tathāgata has understood it.

119
00:08:08,963 --> 00:08:12,284
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I'll continue with the Ajahn Chah passage.

120
00:08:13,525 --> 00:08:15,225
[SPEAKER_04]: It says, most of us have wrong view.

121
00:08:16,846 --> 00:08:17,846
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just like a maggot.

122
00:08:18,567 --> 00:08:22,508
[SPEAKER_04]: A maggot lives... his living place is filthy.

123
00:08:22,708 --> 00:08:23,789
[SPEAKER_04]: Its food is filthy.

124
00:08:24,949 --> 00:08:26,710
[SPEAKER_04]: But they suit a maggot.

125
00:08:27,462 --> 00:08:32,643
[SPEAKER_04]: If you take a stick and brush it away from its lump of dung, it struggles to crawl back in.

126
00:08:33,543 --> 00:08:36,884
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the same when the teacher teaches us to see rightly.

127
00:08:37,904 --> 00:08:38,664
[SPEAKER_04]: We resist it.

128
00:08:38,984 --> 00:08:40,965
[SPEAKER_04]: Make us... it makes us feel uneasy.

129
00:08:42,165 --> 00:08:44,066
[SPEAKER_04]: We run back to our lump of dung.

130
00:08:44,806 --> 00:08:47,646
[SPEAKER_04]: Because of that... because that's where we feel at home.

131
00:08:48,627 --> 00:08:49,487
[SPEAKER_04]: We're all like this.

132
00:08:50,167 --> 00:08:55,088
[SPEAKER_04]: If we don't see the harmful consequences of our wrong views, then we can't leave them.

133
00:08:56,152 --> 00:08:57,113
[SPEAKER_04]: Practice is difficult.

134
00:08:57,493 --> 00:08:58,714
[SPEAKER_04]: So, we should listen.

135
00:08:58,734 --> 00:08:59,895
[SPEAKER_04]: There's nothing else to practice.

136
00:09:00,976 --> 00:09:01,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you should listen.

137
00:09:02,798 --> 00:09:06,061
[SPEAKER_00]: If the one that's talking to you knows what he's talking about.

138
00:09:08,463 --> 00:09:12,286
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't just listen somebody appointed a teacher by the institution of tradition.

139
00:09:13,087 --> 00:09:15,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, good luck with that.

140
00:09:15,329 --> 00:09:18,892
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should listen, but you should also discern who you listen to.

141
00:09:20,153 --> 00:09:21,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, I mean,

142
00:09:22,498 --> 00:09:36,305
[SPEAKER_00]: You can interpret that bit in the same sense, like, you know, when somebody comes as a layman, become a monk, sense restraint means I'm going against my my will, I'm going against my environment of dhamma that I used to be, that I was used to.

143
00:09:37,006 --> 00:09:46,631
[SPEAKER_00]: But the Ādhyācārya talks about the views, not just, not just the sense restraint and, and renunciation, but the actual views.

144
00:09:47,991 --> 00:09:49,372
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, that's where the difference is.

145
00:09:50,001 --> 00:09:53,823
[SPEAKER_00]: How many people actually actively challenge their existential views?

146
00:09:55,144 --> 00:10:01,347
[SPEAKER_00]: How many people, like, invite on the existential doubt in order to uproot it?

147
00:10:03,448 --> 00:10:04,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And not many.

148
00:10:04,969 --> 00:10:08,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people can live a perfectly restrained life, and that's very nice.

149
00:10:09,231 --> 00:10:11,792
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't differentiate you from a restrained householder.

150
00:10:12,413 --> 00:10:13,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Right view does.

151
00:10:15,414 --> 00:10:18,556
[SPEAKER_00]: But for right view, you just being a renunciate, that's not enough.

152
00:10:21,054 --> 00:10:22,375
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how to challenge your views.

153
00:10:22,395 --> 00:10:23,635
[SPEAKER_00]: How to challenge your views, yeah.

154
00:10:24,876 --> 00:10:28,638
[SPEAKER_00]: But not like, oh, I'll just do what the teacher says, which is a common practice in Thailand.

155
00:10:29,118 --> 00:10:32,419
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't read the suttas, I won't think for myself, I just do what Ajahn tells me to do.

156
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:37,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if Ajahn is an arahant himself, that's not enough for you to become one.

157
00:10:38,242 --> 00:10:39,863
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just do what he tells you to do.

158
00:10:41,044 --> 00:10:46,106
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what it said in the... in the... before we spoke about it.

159
00:10:47,807 --> 00:10:50,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just accept it as it is, even if it's your own teacher.

160
00:10:51,946 --> 00:10:53,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there's the other paragraph.

161
00:10:55,268 --> 00:10:56,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should listen.

162
00:10:57,430 --> 00:11:04,115
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have a good faith, based on your own discernment, that what you are listening to is correct, then, yeah, you should listen and make the effort to do it.

163
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:15,704
[SPEAKER_04]: It says, if we have right view, wherever we go, we are content.

164
00:11:16,604 --> 00:11:16,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

165
00:11:18,466 --> 00:11:20,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Because wherever you go, you're out of suffering.

166
00:11:23,110 --> 00:11:25,471
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, as Nyanaviru was talking about, the sense of safety.

167
00:11:25,491 --> 00:11:27,433
[SPEAKER_04]: The safety ground.

168
00:11:27,473 --> 00:11:27,933
[SPEAKER_04]: The safe ground.

169
00:11:53,192 --> 00:11:53,672
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view.

170
00:11:54,012 --> 00:11:55,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not shorter than that.

171
00:11:55,853 --> 00:12:06,319
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep insisting with people, trying to... trying to justify not having the right view, but... but arguing that they're on the right track of their practice.

172
00:12:06,479 --> 00:12:08,080
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only know that if you have the right view.

173
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:13,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, until you have a right view, you shouldn't think you're on the right track, because it will make you complacent to that extent.

174
00:12:16,024 --> 00:12:17,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's exactly what Ajahn Chah is saying there.

175
00:12:18,855 --> 00:12:21,817
[SPEAKER_00]: The only place of safety and coolness is the right view.

176
00:12:22,177 --> 00:12:23,137
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no lower than that.

177
00:12:24,378 --> 00:12:27,399
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are aiming for lower than that, you can expect to be even lower than that.

178
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:35,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should really be aiming for higher than that, to at least get the right view then, as a result of it.

179
00:12:35,163 --> 00:12:36,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me finish these quotations.

180
00:12:36,804 --> 00:12:38,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, finish them.

181
00:12:38,165 --> 00:12:43,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I just come back to that first one, when he said that we should not trust our moods?

182
00:12:44,708 --> 00:12:47,550
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, our moods are also like part of our experience.

183
00:12:49,567 --> 00:12:51,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Not trusting the moods doesn't mean denying them.

184
00:12:52,288 --> 00:12:53,629
[SPEAKER_00]: It means not acting out of them.

185
00:12:54,789 --> 00:12:57,851
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to act out of your mood, you must have taken it for granted.

186
00:12:58,011 --> 00:12:58,211
[SPEAKER_03]: Same.

187
00:12:58,391 --> 00:13:01,112
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to act out of sensuality, you took it at a face value.

188
00:13:01,652 --> 00:13:04,133
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to act out of ill-will, you took it at a face value.

189
00:13:04,293 --> 00:13:04,954
[SPEAKER_00]: You trust it.

190
00:13:05,834 --> 00:13:08,995
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not trusting it doesn't mean, oh, I'm not accepting this.

191
00:13:09,015 --> 00:13:10,276
[SPEAKER_00]: It means I don't act out of it.

192
00:13:11,056 --> 00:13:17,859
[SPEAKER_00]: It means I'm aware of it in its persistence, basically, for what it is, and I'm not trusting it to make me act and do things out of it.

193
00:13:31,067 --> 00:13:34,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how the another thing that people keep quoting on which everything is teaching us.

194
00:13:35,168 --> 00:13:36,428
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how everything teaches you.

195
00:13:36,888 --> 00:13:39,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of your attitude in regard to everything.

196
00:13:39,849 --> 00:13:42,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because everything is actually teaching you.

197
00:13:42,390 --> 00:13:42,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

198
00:13:46,971 --> 00:13:54,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Which basically again brings that thing that we must put the effort to understand or to practice that the teaching is about the...

199
00:13:54,953 --> 00:13:56,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature of things means anything.

200
00:13:57,608 --> 00:13:58,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Partakes in that nature.

201
00:13:58,948 --> 00:14:02,750
[SPEAKER_00]: If you discern in the nature, means anything will be partaking in your discernment.

202
00:14:08,932 --> 00:14:14,534
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say, basically, when you say, like, you don't, if you don't have right view, you don't know.

203
00:14:15,114 --> 00:14:21,256
[SPEAKER_01]: But this can actually put everyone who has no right view in the position of, like, you know, like, I don't know.

204
00:14:22,637 --> 00:14:26,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and then he can, he can be, or that person can be, like,

205
00:14:30,335 --> 00:14:40,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I'm not confident actually to express anything, to do anything, and just trying to follow someone, because you would always say, you know, I don't know, I still don't have right view.

206
00:14:40,423 --> 00:14:45,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but following, following someone, that person would be fully aware that I have no right view.

207
00:14:47,269 --> 00:14:52,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, following someone on account of me not having a right view, has nothing to do with me not having the right view or having the right view.

208
00:14:53,915 --> 00:14:56,357
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they would not be justified in following someone.

209
00:14:56,397 --> 00:14:57,698
[SPEAKER_00]: It wouldn't make them follow someone.

210
00:14:58,050 --> 00:15:09,476
[SPEAKER_01]: But what I mean is more like, you know, then there can be a person who is just claiming, yes, I have right view, and all these unconfident people who don't have right view, who just go and follow him.

211
00:15:09,656 --> 00:15:11,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that's because they don't have the right view.

212
00:15:11,657 --> 00:15:12,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's exactly.

213
00:15:13,518 --> 00:15:14,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not what I'm saying.

214
00:15:15,159 --> 00:15:16,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what this is saying either.

215
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:17,521
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just quite the opposite.

216
00:15:17,941 --> 00:15:19,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone else's right view doesn't do anything for you.

217
00:15:22,337 --> 00:15:23,978
[SPEAKER_00]: That's even what the first quote said.

218
00:15:24,538 --> 00:15:29,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's your own teacher, as in somebody who doesn't know what he's talking about, you must investigate that.

219
00:15:29,240 --> 00:15:30,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just follow it.

220
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:31,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just take it.

221
00:15:31,681 --> 00:15:35,362
[SPEAKER_01]: But the person who is like following the teaching of the Buddha, he has basically right view.

222
00:15:36,093 --> 00:15:36,233
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

223
00:15:36,353 --> 00:15:37,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpaṭṭhāna has the right view.

224
00:15:37,574 --> 00:15:41,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but you have also this lokīyā right view, this ordinary right view.

225
00:15:42,037 --> 00:15:42,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but that's...

226
00:15:42,718 --> 00:15:44,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you are not ārya, so you actually don't...

227
00:15:44,599 --> 00:15:45,680
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the right view.

228
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:48,882
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a right view, but... No, it's a mundane right view.

229
00:15:48,942 --> 00:15:49,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, mundane right view.

230
00:15:49,562 --> 00:15:51,404
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm talking about sotāpaṭṭhāna, freedom from suffering.

231
00:15:51,464 --> 00:15:53,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Mundane right view doesn't free you from suffering.

232
00:15:54,032 --> 00:15:55,472
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still kind of right view.

233
00:15:55,532 --> 00:15:56,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

234
00:15:56,693 --> 00:15:59,494
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a right basis for the right view of the subject.

235
00:15:59,654 --> 00:16:01,915
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view that matters is the sotāpanna's right view.

236
00:16:02,475 --> 00:16:04,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, it doesn't mean that lay right view doesn't matter.

237
00:16:05,316 --> 00:16:08,397
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you can't have sotāpanna's right view without the mundane right view.

238
00:16:08,957 --> 00:16:11,458
[SPEAKER_00]: But mundane right view, you're still a putujuna.

239
00:16:12,458 --> 00:16:14,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And the sense of safety starts with sotāpanna.

240
00:16:15,379 --> 00:16:15,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

241
00:16:15,959 --> 00:16:16,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Not with putujuna.

242
00:16:16,819 --> 00:16:19,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mundane right view is not the sense of safety.

243
00:16:19,887 --> 00:16:23,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so we are speaking about this ārya right view, not the ordinary... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

244
00:16:24,029 --> 00:16:24,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

245
00:16:24,329 --> 00:16:25,289
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's what I said there.

246
00:16:26,449 --> 00:16:35,573
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, we can speak about the mundane right view and you realize most people don't... most monks don't even have it, not even mundane right view, despite being in robes for 40-50 years.

247
00:16:36,453 --> 00:16:37,033
[SPEAKER_01]: You think so?

248
00:16:37,354 --> 00:16:38,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, most certainly.

249
00:16:38,454 --> 00:16:45,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you start thinking about the mundane right view, you realize it's already quite an accomplishment in undoing lots of wrong views.

250
00:16:45,817 --> 00:16:48,858
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite a lot of self-transparency developed through mundane right view.

251
00:16:49,078 --> 00:16:49,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

252
00:16:49,725 --> 00:16:52,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognition of the nature of things through mundane right view.

253
00:16:52,887 --> 00:16:59,732
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you hear the the other people, monks, whatever, speaking, you realize they don't even have the basic recognition of those views.

254
00:17:00,112 --> 00:17:00,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

255
00:17:00,852 --> 00:17:05,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, please, can you actually speak about this mundane right view, you know?

256
00:17:05,435 --> 00:17:06,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we spoke before.

257
00:17:06,456 --> 00:17:11,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember, I think, I think it was with you that I spoke about the authenticity and self-transparency.

258
00:17:11,960 --> 00:17:13,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's... That's exactly what it is.

259
00:17:15,022 --> 00:17:15,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Like,

260
00:17:16,633 --> 00:17:19,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognition of the validity of the risen phenomena.

261
00:17:21,135 --> 00:17:31,299
[SPEAKER_00]: There is given, there is good, there is bad, there is mother, there is father, there is the fact that I don't know something.

262
00:17:31,519 --> 00:17:33,140
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the next life, there is this life.

263
00:17:33,240 --> 00:17:41,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, all these things is basically a recognition of the state of mind as it is, not coming out of a view that you have preconceived.

264
00:17:43,340 --> 00:17:47,324
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, there is rebirth, because I believe in it, you know, because I see it.

265
00:17:47,744 --> 00:17:51,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, there is no rebirth, because I don't believe in it, not because I see it.

266
00:17:52,869 --> 00:17:54,350
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize it, I don't know.

267
00:17:54,951 --> 00:17:57,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first bit for me, it's not there is rebirth, there isn't.

268
00:17:57,313 --> 00:17:58,574
[SPEAKER_00]: There is lack of knowledge.

269
00:17:59,375 --> 00:18:00,656
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a factual thing.

270
00:18:01,156 --> 00:18:02,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize it as an arisen thing.

271
00:18:04,532 --> 00:18:07,013
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know, like, people might say, oh, yeah, there is mother, there is father.

272
00:18:07,373 --> 00:18:14,017
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to ask them, they would jump on to the view of modern science and medicine and explaining that it's just a bunch of molecules and electrons.

273
00:18:14,037 --> 00:18:15,137
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing in reality.

274
00:18:15,157 --> 00:18:15,457
[SPEAKER_00]: It's real.

275
00:18:15,497 --> 00:18:16,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is constantly changing.

276
00:18:17,300 --> 00:18:26,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see that fully committed Buddhist people and practitioner undermine the basic reality of the world that they've experienced themselves and that you can't, like, avoid it.

277
00:18:26,507 --> 00:18:28,529
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can certainly misinterpret it with your view.

278
00:18:28,749 --> 00:18:30,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means they don't even have a mundane right view.

279
00:18:31,111 --> 00:18:35,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Because their views deny the basic mundane aspects of their experience.

280
00:18:36,935 --> 00:18:39,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what it means there is mother, there is father.

281
00:18:39,417 --> 00:18:39,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no.

282
00:18:39,998 --> 00:18:40,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Mother is mother.

283
00:18:41,419 --> 00:18:41,759
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

284
00:18:42,340 --> 00:18:44,962
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no lower threshold than that in your experience.

285
00:18:46,285 --> 00:18:56,970
[SPEAKER_00]: But people might talk about it on some occasion, but fundamentally, their view would be like an external assumption of a scientific world, for example, which says, well, in reality, we are just, you know, lumps of flesh.

286
00:18:57,090 --> 00:18:57,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing's real.

287
00:18:58,030 --> 00:18:59,671
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why everything is constantly impermanent.

288
00:19:00,413 --> 00:19:02,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's nothing to do with Buddhist teaching whatsoever.

289
00:19:02,655 --> 00:19:02,775
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

290
00:19:02,815 --> 00:19:04,336
[SPEAKER_00]: There is mother, there is father.

291
00:19:04,816 --> 00:19:05,517
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your threshold.

292
00:19:05,537 --> 00:19:06,318
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your starting point.

293
00:19:06,878 --> 00:19:18,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the impermanence you discern within that, it's that impermanence of ownership, impermanence of appropriation, impossibility to kind of be a controlled master of things, because they were risen on their own.

294
00:19:18,527 --> 00:19:20,969
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you realize, ah, it's the impermanence of my sense of self.

295
00:19:21,389 --> 00:19:24,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Not impermanence of a random stone, tree, table, and so on.

296
00:19:26,443 --> 00:19:33,465
[SPEAKER_00]: And how many times, I bet you heard yourself, people say that, yeah, modern science is proving that the Buddha was correct, because everything is constantly changing.

297
00:19:33,565 --> 00:19:37,546
[SPEAKER_00]: And you hear that from very, very senior established monks in their talks and whatnot.

298
00:19:38,046 --> 00:19:45,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, yeah, you can be in ropes for the rest of your life, hundred years, and still not even have a mundane right view, let alone supplementary.

299
00:19:46,428 --> 00:19:47,548
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's no one else's fault.

300
00:19:48,888 --> 00:19:51,409
[SPEAKER_00]: You are always solely responsible for the views you hold.

301
00:19:54,410 --> 00:19:55,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognition of

302
00:19:56,542 --> 00:20:00,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, of things arising on their own, to what extent they arise.

303
00:20:00,803 --> 00:20:03,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognition of things as phenomena, if you want to call it that way.

304
00:20:04,085 --> 00:20:10,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Not jump into material explanation, not mystify it in the ideological explanation, but literally just know.

305
00:20:11,347 --> 00:20:13,088
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the experience of having a mother.

306
00:20:14,128 --> 00:20:15,949
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the experience of having a father.

307
00:20:16,469 --> 00:20:17,869
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the experience of doubt.

308
00:20:18,509 --> 00:20:20,070
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the experience of sensuality.

309
00:20:20,630 --> 00:20:25,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Already withdraw from that material content of it, that we are all so focused on.

310
00:20:26,974 --> 00:20:31,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the necessary basis for really discerning the signs of your mind as phenomena.

311
00:20:32,038 --> 00:20:33,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the problem.

312
00:20:33,719 --> 00:20:38,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Having these views prevents you from seeing your mind as a thing that has arisen.

313
00:20:39,203 --> 00:20:42,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is an absolute prerequisite for the right view, as the Buddha said.

314
00:20:42,326 --> 00:20:43,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Citta, nimitta.

315
00:20:43,286 --> 00:20:44,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Without it, you can't have the right view.

316
00:20:45,308 --> 00:20:45,588
[SPEAKER_00]: I see.

317
00:20:45,928 --> 00:20:51,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you see your mind as an enduring thing, when obviously enduring things you deny with your own view?

318
00:20:54,410 --> 00:21:00,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need the mundane right view to get used to it and then to start seeing things that matter.

319
00:21:00,714 --> 00:21:04,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind, feeling, intentions, on the level of enduring things.

320
00:21:06,257 --> 00:21:09,278
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why many people struggle with those three characteristics that the Buddha describes.

321
00:21:09,658 --> 00:21:13,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifest, ceasing is manifest, resisting while changing is manifest.

322
00:21:13,621 --> 00:21:13,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

323
00:21:14,081 --> 00:21:17,123
[SPEAKER_00]: People read that, but they always keep saying and keep holding the view.

324
00:21:17,463 --> 00:21:21,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifest, persisting while changing is manifest, ceasing is manifest.

325
00:21:22,065 --> 00:21:23,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they change the order.

326
00:21:23,513 --> 00:21:28,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for them, it's all seen in the third person of me observing something lasting and then disappearing.

327
00:21:28,738 --> 00:21:30,239
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

328
00:21:30,259 --> 00:21:31,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifest.

329
00:21:32,241 --> 00:21:33,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Ceasing is manifest.

330
00:21:34,263 --> 00:21:35,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Persisting while changing is manifest.

331
00:21:37,846 --> 00:21:41,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already something that's not linear in the way people are used to.

332
00:21:42,163 --> 00:21:48,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if linear observance is required for you to understand impermanence, all you need to do is wait for your body to die.

333
00:21:48,964 --> 00:21:50,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Death will be the enlightenment.

334
00:21:51,005 --> 00:21:51,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

335
00:21:52,025 --> 00:21:55,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation is another arising, another manifestation.

336
00:21:56,326 --> 00:21:58,646
[SPEAKER_00]: But for people, cessation means the impermanence.

337
00:21:58,686 --> 00:21:59,067
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't.

338
00:22:00,047 --> 00:22:01,627
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha said millions of times.

339
00:22:01,767 --> 00:22:03,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifested.

340
00:22:04,588 --> 00:22:06,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Ceasing is manifested.

341
00:22:08,129 --> 00:22:12,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, thing endures, and that enduring is also manifested.

342
00:22:12,472 --> 00:22:13,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Resisting while remaining the same.

343
00:22:14,053 --> 00:22:16,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And it relates to this, like, there is mother...

344
00:22:16,115 --> 00:22:17,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

345
00:22:17,176 --> 00:22:27,924
[SPEAKER_00]: A certain threshold that you don't overly, kind of, eh... overly determine with your interpretation of science and medicine, but you don't go the other way and then mystify it.

346
00:22:27,964 --> 00:22:29,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Then say everything is constantly whatever.

347
00:22:30,966 --> 00:22:32,727
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you recognize there is the arising.

348
00:22:33,248 --> 00:22:33,988
[SPEAKER_00]: It's manifested.

349
00:22:34,589 --> 00:22:36,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there is the experience of cessation.

350
00:22:36,710 --> 00:22:37,451
[SPEAKER_00]: It's manifested.

351
00:22:38,636 --> 00:22:41,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, things are enduring.

352
00:22:41,679 --> 00:22:44,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Either in the form of arising or ceasing, they're enduring.

353
00:22:45,363 --> 00:22:46,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's manifested as well.

354
00:22:47,104 --> 00:22:47,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

355
00:22:49,086 --> 00:22:51,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And we are basically caught in this manifestation.

356
00:22:51,969 --> 00:22:53,931
[SPEAKER_01]: That is... You can't step outside of it.

357
00:22:53,971 --> 00:22:54,332
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't...

358
00:22:54,392 --> 00:22:54,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

359
00:22:55,052 --> 00:23:00,097
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is you assuming the external point of view to it, which ultimately is what attavāda is.

360
00:23:00,117 --> 00:23:02,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming the external self to this whole thing.

361
00:23:03,750 --> 00:23:06,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you basically can't step outside of manifestation.

362
00:23:06,672 --> 00:23:06,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

363
00:23:06,873 --> 00:23:08,734
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what defines wrong view as a wrong view.

364
00:23:08,754 --> 00:23:12,457
[SPEAKER_00]: A view that contradicts the fundamental universal aspect of your experience.

365
00:23:12,978 --> 00:23:16,380
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha did not invent the right view, did not invent the dhamma.

366
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:18,102
[SPEAKER_00]: He just discerned it on the right terms.

367
00:23:18,922 --> 00:23:20,604
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, for that you need instruction.

368
00:23:20,624 --> 00:23:24,287
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't accidentally arrive at it, because everything points in the wrong direction.

369
00:23:29,029 --> 00:23:29,189
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

370
00:23:29,209 --> 00:23:29,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

371
00:23:29,529 --> 00:23:30,450
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's what I mean.

372
00:23:30,470 --> 00:23:33,552
[SPEAKER_02]: Mundane right view recognizes the the the basis.

373
00:23:58,322 --> 00:24:00,403
[SPEAKER_00]: There is an experience as a starting point.

374
00:24:01,484 --> 00:24:07,587
[SPEAKER_00]: See, like, you have there is an experience of emotionally charged experience of your father getting sick or something.

375
00:24:09,248 --> 00:24:10,009
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the base.

376
00:24:10,069 --> 00:24:11,149
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the starting point.

377
00:24:11,249 --> 00:24:17,093
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see, even most people's practice is about covering that up, denying it, removing it.

378
00:24:17,453 --> 00:24:17,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

379
00:24:17,853 --> 00:24:18,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it bothers me.

380
00:24:19,694 --> 00:24:23,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, my practice becomes management of what bothers me.

381
00:24:23,796 --> 00:24:25,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And you manage it through the wrong views.

382
00:24:25,557 --> 00:24:26,558
[SPEAKER_00]: You manage it through saying

383
00:24:27,297 --> 00:24:29,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, fundamentally, it's just a collection of atoms.

384
00:24:29,959 --> 00:24:32,640
[SPEAKER_00]: And what... that's another way of saying, fundamentally, there is no father.

385
00:24:33,641 --> 00:24:35,001
[SPEAKER_00]: In reality, there is no father.

386
00:24:35,041 --> 00:24:36,842
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just my attachment that the father is there.

387
00:24:36,922 --> 00:24:37,423
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

388
00:24:38,263 --> 00:24:39,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Your father is there.

389
00:24:39,204 --> 00:24:40,124
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your starting point.

390
00:24:41,145 --> 00:24:44,847
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start thinking like that, then your practice of dhamma cannot be the management.

391
00:24:45,347 --> 00:24:48,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only result in uprooting of the problem in the first place.

392
00:24:51,210 --> 00:24:56,513
[SPEAKER_00]: If your practice is secondary, means it's management, which means it's not the dhamma, even if you do the dhamma on account of it.

393
00:24:58,720 --> 00:25:01,503
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you have no leeway of denying the fatherhood.

394
00:25:02,624 --> 00:25:04,026
[SPEAKER_00]: The experience of there is a father.

395
00:25:04,606 --> 00:25:05,928
[SPEAKER_00]: The experience of there is a mother.

396
00:25:06,248 --> 00:25:08,110
[SPEAKER_00]: The experience there is an experience of doubt.

397
00:25:08,751 --> 00:25:09,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't know that.

398
00:25:11,493 --> 00:25:14,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the reason factually enduring present states of your mind.

399
00:25:16,867 --> 00:25:25,693
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stop denying it, then you realize the only way to not be bothered by it, is to uproot the craving towards the feeling, as in remove the suffering.

400
00:25:26,214 --> 00:25:29,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through saying, everything is already impermanent, thus I shouldn't suffer.

401
00:25:30,377 --> 00:25:35,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, it's just a collection of flesh organs and constantly moving electrons, there is no father.

402
00:25:36,081 --> 00:25:38,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Through denying it, you try to find a way out of what bothers you.

403
00:25:40,576 --> 00:25:41,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean.

404
00:25:41,597 --> 00:25:44,138
[SPEAKER_00]: It's management of the fact that you're bothered in the first place.

405
00:25:44,779 --> 00:25:47,460
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you do dhamma rightly, you can't be bothered in the first place.

406
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:51,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there is no reason for you to deny your father, mother, divine realms, whatever.

407
00:25:51,983 --> 00:25:52,663
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no reason.

408
00:25:53,264 --> 00:25:53,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you?

409
00:25:53,784 --> 00:25:57,746
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason people deny it is because they don't know other way of... other way out of suffering.

410
00:26:00,068 --> 00:26:02,909
[SPEAKER_01]: And Bhante, what about this experience of kama and vipāka?

411
00:26:02,949 --> 00:26:04,110
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't have this experience.

412
00:26:04,437 --> 00:26:05,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, see, that's another thing.

413
00:26:05,658 --> 00:26:08,320
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha never said, like, you must figure it...

414
00:26:08,340 --> 00:26:12,103
[SPEAKER_00]: He actually said, thinking about karma vipāka will result in distraction or madness.

415
00:26:12,744 --> 00:26:14,125
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what you recognize.

416
00:26:14,865 --> 00:26:18,568
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know to what extent my actions will bear fruit.

417
00:26:18,688 --> 00:26:22,191
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your fact... that's your factual experience right there and then.

418
00:26:23,052 --> 00:26:24,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you act then?

419
00:26:24,653 --> 00:26:33,400
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not dismissing karma, or if you don't have, like, a perfectly cut kind of template of how karma works, you realize, oh, crap, I don't know.

420
00:26:34,242 --> 00:26:40,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I better be really, really careful, because even when I think I might be doing good, I don't know for myself.

421
00:26:41,744 --> 00:26:43,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not worth the risk.

422
00:26:44,125 --> 00:26:49,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's same when people ask him about the next life, and the Buddha said, well, the truth is you don't know.

423
00:26:49,927 --> 00:26:50,807
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your starting point.

424
00:26:50,967 --> 00:26:51,707
[SPEAKER_00]: But you reflect.

425
00:26:51,887 --> 00:26:52,368
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

426
00:26:52,788 --> 00:26:57,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I better act in the best-case scenario, in the worst-case scenario, just in case that it is true.

427
00:26:58,709 --> 00:26:59,990
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be too much to handle.

428
00:27:00,718 --> 00:27:04,379
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll assume there is, because it will make me act carefully.

429
00:27:04,979 --> 00:27:06,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, win-win.

430
00:27:07,199 --> 00:27:08,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, win-win situation.

431
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:17,221
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, it's basically a win-win situation, because you are preparing the ground for a rising of this...

432
00:27:17,421 --> 00:27:17,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

433
00:27:17,901 --> 00:27:18,541
[SPEAKER_01]: ...super mundane right view.

434
00:27:18,561 --> 00:27:19,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely, yeah.

435
00:27:19,481 --> 00:27:19,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

436
00:27:20,601 --> 00:27:28,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Because without that mundane, you cannot... No, the only reason people don't have the even mundane right view, is because they haven't taken up responsibility for their own existence.

437
00:27:28,983 --> 00:27:29,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

438
00:27:30,453 --> 00:27:36,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as I said, you can be a perfectly restrained monk for 60 years and still have not taken responsibility for your existence.

439
00:27:37,535 --> 00:27:43,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you have tradition doing the responsibility for you, vinaya doing the responsibility for you, form doing the responsibility for you.

440
00:27:44,598 --> 00:27:46,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, you must take it on.

441
00:27:46,718 --> 00:27:47,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You must do it.

442
00:27:48,219 --> 00:27:51,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Which initially is going to result, oh, so I could then decide to not do it.

443
00:27:52,893 --> 00:27:55,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, a whim of thought can uproot your whole existence.

444
00:27:56,134 --> 00:27:57,394
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's very, very scary.

445
00:27:58,175 --> 00:28:01,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you just move from one authority, placing you into the next authority.

446
00:28:02,436 --> 00:28:09,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Until, eventually, you go into the authority of old age, senile experience of, like, you know, not being bothered by anything, because you can't remember half of the things.

447
00:28:10,438 --> 00:28:12,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you say, ah, old age is so graceful and peaceful.

448
00:28:13,959 --> 00:28:18,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, sexual desire doesn't bother you, because you just went completely dormant, and you can't even bother thinking about it.

449
00:28:19,461 --> 00:28:20,081
[SPEAKER_00]: So, good luck.

450
00:28:20,947 --> 00:28:34,180
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not uprooting anything at that stage, if you haven't uprooted things when they were obvious, when you could have developed transparency, you're not going to do it after hammering them down with, like, decades of habits of wrong views.

451
00:28:41,878 --> 00:28:49,483
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you think is the balance between, like, you know, we can... we have to obviously face doubts, because we don't know certain things.

452
00:28:50,444 --> 00:29:02,472
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the difference between, like, getting kind of, eh... strewn around by those doubts, and you kind of just start, eh... going the other way, versus using your doubt in a way that can be useful?

453
00:29:02,913 --> 00:29:06,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the difference is whether you are aware of the presently enduring nature of the doubt.

454
00:29:07,477 --> 00:29:15,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you doubt the content of something, but you can't doubt the experience of doubt being present.

455
00:29:15,383 --> 00:29:22,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the best way to withdraw yourself from any involvement with content of your experience is the doubt.

456
00:29:24,030 --> 00:29:26,332
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where sātras, being in nothingness, starts.

457
00:29:26,392 --> 00:29:29,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, being becomes revealed when you put yourself in question.

458
00:29:30,755 --> 00:29:33,237
[SPEAKER_00]: You start questioning your own existence, that's it.

459
00:29:33,457 --> 00:29:35,299
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you arrive at your own existence.

460
00:29:36,319 --> 00:29:37,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Through questioning it.

461
00:29:38,072 --> 00:29:40,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like academically, genuinely.

462
00:29:41,214 --> 00:29:44,256
[SPEAKER_00]: I am, but I could have not been.

463
00:29:44,296 --> 00:29:46,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Immediately withdraws you from the entire content.

464
00:29:47,058 --> 00:29:54,523
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understand the nature of that, means you have fully transcended any form of pull that content can exercise over you.

465
00:29:55,083 --> 00:29:59,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can doubt this, you can doubt that, but you can't doubt the experience of the present.

466
00:29:59,206 --> 00:30:00,927
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't doubt in regard to this content.

467
00:30:02,692 --> 00:30:03,733
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the Buddha said.

468
00:30:03,753 --> 00:30:07,438
[SPEAKER_00]: The best of the worst of the wrong views is that one, eh...

469
00:30:08,118 --> 00:30:08,759
[SPEAKER_00]: I could have...

470
00:30:08,799 --> 00:30:09,820
[SPEAKER_00]: I could have been... No.

471
00:30:09,860 --> 00:30:10,441
[SPEAKER_00]: It could have been...

472
00:30:10,521 --> 00:30:11,623
[SPEAKER_00]: It could have not been for me.

473
00:30:11,703 --> 00:30:12,383
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not be.

474
00:30:12,724 --> 00:30:16,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally withdrawn from any content, any possibility of the content.

475
00:30:17,009 --> 00:30:19,992
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you will let go of that, that's ārahaṅca.

476
00:30:20,793 --> 00:30:22,235
[SPEAKER_00]: But for that, you will need the right view.

477
00:30:23,665 --> 00:30:30,590
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, it's a good thing, even with the wrong view, to develop it, because it already primes you very closely to the right view.

478
00:30:30,790 --> 00:30:38,616
[SPEAKER_00]: You already are on the level of recognizing the universal aspect of the enduring experience, not being pulled by the content of it.

479
00:30:39,837 --> 00:30:48,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes doubt will affect you more, doubt will affect you less, but you will know that you are more or less on the right tracks, if you remain aware of the present enduring doubt as a state of mind.

480
00:30:49,723 --> 00:31:00,726
[SPEAKER_03]: But I've been trying to think about when things like all these kinds of states of doubt, frustration, despair, trying to think about things, like, it's a question of, but am I, am I, how bothered am I by it in the first place?

481
00:31:01,266 --> 00:31:06,108
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's, your mind kind of tells you that, like, it's almost like you're obligated to be bothered.

482
00:31:06,128 --> 00:31:08,548
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if you're not bothered, you're not doing the effort.

483
00:31:08,588 --> 00:31:09,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

484
00:31:09,348 --> 00:31:10,789
[SPEAKER_00]: I must, I must do something, yeah.

485
00:31:11,109 --> 00:31:14,510
[SPEAKER_00]: But then it's like, what if not being bothered by this is the effort?

486
00:31:15,474 --> 00:31:16,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not dismissing it.

487
00:31:16,595 --> 00:31:17,456
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not denying it.

488
00:31:17,536 --> 00:31:18,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there is no doubt.

489
00:31:18,336 --> 00:31:18,877
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing.

490
00:31:18,897 --> 00:31:19,457
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no mother.

491
00:31:19,477 --> 00:31:20,138
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no father.

492
00:31:20,858 --> 00:31:23,621
[SPEAKER_00]: But, at the same time, you're recognizing, okay, it has arisen.

493
00:31:23,801 --> 00:31:24,982
[SPEAKER_00]: It's real to that extent.

494
00:31:26,183 --> 00:31:29,946
[SPEAKER_00]: But, now, the choice is you need to give in to the craving towards the content or not.

495
00:31:30,927 --> 00:31:33,409
[SPEAKER_00]: And, that's how you find that middle ground.

496
00:31:33,429 --> 00:31:39,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither completely dismissing it, nor completely giving in to it and following it, sort of, off with course.

497
00:31:43,894 --> 00:31:48,852
[SPEAKER_04]: Part of it's mundane right view, at the end of its passage.

498
00:31:50,020 --> 00:31:55,562
[SPEAKER_04]: It says, there are those who have realized for themselves direct knowledge and declare this world and the other world.

499
00:31:55,622 --> 00:31:59,244
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there are those who have realized, yeah, who know.

500
00:32:00,484 --> 00:32:01,785
[SPEAKER_04]: That's mundane, like you.

501
00:32:02,245 --> 00:32:04,926
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you know that it's possible.

502
00:32:05,026 --> 00:32:07,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the extent, the extent of the possibility.

503
00:32:07,767 --> 00:32:07,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

504
00:32:07,987 --> 00:32:13,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, like possibility, even for most people, even their own thoughts would be regarded as something that's not real.

505
00:32:14,803 --> 00:32:16,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, because they're used to substance.

506
00:32:16,785 --> 00:32:21,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Their view, the level of being, the level of proliferated consciousness, is on the level of substance.

507
00:32:21,928 --> 00:32:25,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when their thoughts are there in the background, it's usually, oh, that's not even real.

508
00:32:26,772 --> 00:32:31,696
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you can call it not real, means there is something that can be called not real, which means that thing is real.

509
00:32:32,456 --> 00:32:36,399
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your view of this is not real, or it's less real, it's a complete contradiction in terms.

510
00:32:37,260 --> 00:32:41,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you completely undo that, you will recognize that possibilities are real as such.

511
00:32:42,653 --> 00:32:50,621
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that extent, you understood that extent of this world, of the next world, of the current world, of all the possible worlds.

512
00:32:53,644 --> 00:32:55,987
[SPEAKER_04]: So, coming to the knowledge that, oh, it's possible.

513
00:32:56,007 --> 00:32:58,629
[SPEAKER_04]: Someone says it's possible to be free from suffering.

514
00:32:58,649 --> 00:32:59,210
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, exactly.

515
00:32:59,310 --> 00:32:59,750
[SPEAKER_04]: Completely.

516
00:33:00,411 --> 00:33:04,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that possibility is present in everyone's experience right here, right now.

517
00:33:05,036 --> 00:33:06,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And always is, always was.

518
00:33:07,165 --> 00:33:07,945
[SPEAKER_00]: And will be as well.

519
00:33:08,385 --> 00:33:09,165
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, what do you do?

520
00:33:09,646 --> 00:33:10,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

521
00:33:10,506 --> 00:33:10,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh!

522
00:33:11,346 --> 00:33:13,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is mother, there's father.

523
00:33:13,306 --> 00:33:14,807
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the possibility of freedom.

524
00:33:15,407 --> 00:33:16,907
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the possibility of the right view.

525
00:33:17,247 --> 00:33:18,628
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the possibility of arahantship.

526
00:33:19,728 --> 00:33:21,668
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, there's no... there's no possibility.

527
00:33:21,848 --> 00:33:23,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what I mean.

528
00:33:23,329 --> 00:33:27,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, almost automatically, that's what I said when you read the previous passage.

529
00:33:27,670 --> 00:33:32,371
[SPEAKER_00]: You start denying this basic... basic manifestation of things.

530
00:33:34,288 --> 00:33:42,677
[SPEAKER_00]: You start justifying it with your external views, you will automatically arrive at the view of there's nothing to be attained anyway, or nothing can be really attained.

531
00:33:43,197 --> 00:33:49,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I heard monks, many different monks, different seniorities, whatever, just openly say those things on dhamma sīla.

532
00:33:50,144 --> 00:33:52,106
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, don't worry about it.

533
00:33:52,126 --> 00:33:53,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's just, that's just more attachment.

534
00:33:54,209 --> 00:33:55,450
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing to be attained.

535
00:33:55,810 --> 00:33:57,452
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you practice, the less you know.

536
00:33:58,562 --> 00:34:02,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe your practice makes you more ignorant, but not what the Buddha was teaching.

537
00:34:02,943 --> 00:34:04,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Not what Ajahn Chah was teaching, for Christ's sake.

538
00:34:05,423 --> 00:34:07,044
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you read his verses, it's quite clear.

539
00:34:07,704 --> 00:34:08,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, don't take it for granted.

540
00:34:09,224 --> 00:34:09,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't dismiss it.

541
00:34:10,524 --> 00:34:13,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand it, and then you'll know it, at least to that extent.

542
00:34:14,345 --> 00:34:16,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, take it further, if it's not sufficient.

543
00:34:17,586 --> 00:34:19,786
[SPEAKER_00]: But, oh, okay, nothing can be trusted.

544
00:34:20,106 --> 00:34:21,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay, I don't trust anything.

545
00:34:21,667 --> 00:34:22,527
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my view now.

546
00:34:22,787 --> 00:34:23,667
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what pleases me.

547
00:34:24,307 --> 00:34:25,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And, uh, what sotāpanna?

548
00:34:25,968 --> 00:34:27,488
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, there's nothing to be attained anyway

549
00:34:28,297 --> 00:34:28,637
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

550
00:34:29,578 --> 00:34:30,078
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

551
00:34:30,118 --> 00:34:39,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally, through denial of that, naturally, you're going to deny everything, and you're not even... you won't even feel responsible for not becoming a Sotāpanna.

552
00:34:39,164 --> 00:34:41,425
[SPEAKER_00]: And do you think there is a connection, maybe, between the two?

553
00:34:41,645 --> 00:34:42,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's too hard to do it?

554
00:34:42,746 --> 00:34:43,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me dismiss it.

555
00:34:43,726 --> 00:34:45,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't feel obliged to do it.

556
00:34:45,828 --> 00:34:46,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

557
00:34:47,549 --> 00:34:56,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, even the Ajahn Chah himself said, if you're not... if you don't have the right view within the first five years, you're wasting your time.

558
00:34:58,821 --> 00:35:00,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but no, there's nothing to be attained.

559
00:35:00,582 --> 00:35:01,503
[SPEAKER_00]: But I follow Ajahn Chah.

560
00:35:01,923 --> 00:35:02,864
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's nothing to be attained.

561
00:35:03,505 --> 00:35:04,625
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no Suddhāpati, nothing.

562
00:35:04,866 --> 00:35:06,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But I also follow the Buddha, and I'm a Buddhist monk.

563
00:35:07,888 --> 00:35:09,249
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, there's nothing to be attained.

564
00:35:14,843 --> 00:35:15,263
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

565
00:35:15,283 --> 00:35:18,545
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes down, mundane reality comes down to accepting that responsibility.

566
00:35:19,605 --> 00:35:21,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is possibility of enlightenment.

567
00:35:21,886 --> 00:35:21,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh!

568
00:35:22,747 --> 00:35:24,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's my possibility of enlightenment.

569
00:35:25,048 --> 00:35:27,469
[SPEAKER_00]: It's possibility of my awakening.

570
00:35:27,849 --> 00:35:30,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm responsible for it then.

571
00:35:30,290 --> 00:35:31,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a possibility to be free.

572
00:35:32,911 --> 00:35:34,752
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm perfectly free.

573
00:35:35,403 --> 00:35:37,184
[SPEAKER_00]: To choose to not go that way.

574
00:35:37,764 --> 00:35:40,925
[SPEAKER_00]: But that means I will be responsible for not getting the results of it.

575
00:35:41,505 --> 00:35:44,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the first thing that hit me when I started reading the suttas as a layman.

576
00:35:45,226 --> 00:35:47,427
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha says, there is suffering, there is freedom from it.

577
00:35:47,467 --> 00:35:48,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Come and see for yourself.

578
00:35:49,307 --> 00:35:52,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remember, because I couldn't even find anybody say that.

579
00:35:52,689 --> 00:35:54,129
[SPEAKER_00]: And now I found somebody said it.

580
00:35:55,049 --> 00:35:57,090
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remember it was finally somebody said it.

581
00:35:57,410 --> 00:36:00,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And I felt it like, I don't have to follow it.

582
00:36:01,927 --> 00:36:03,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I know there is, but I could go the other way.

583
00:36:04,188 --> 00:36:14,532
[SPEAKER_00]: But I immediately I felt like, okay, so if I choose to not become a monk and practice this, I am fully responsible for whatever happens to me for the rest of my life, even if it's not my fault in lay terms.

584
00:36:15,653 --> 00:36:17,793
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the world, there is freedom from the world.

585
00:36:18,314 --> 00:36:23,596
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose the world, means you choose everything else that might come on account of that world.

586
00:36:23,676 --> 00:36:26,357
[SPEAKER_00]: It's based on that choice of you not renouncing it.

587
00:36:27,648 --> 00:36:27,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And that...

588
00:36:28,168 --> 00:36:28,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't ignore that.

589
00:36:29,089 --> 00:36:34,852
[SPEAKER_00]: I could not ignore that fundamentally, then, I'm responsible even for complete things that are completely out of your control.

590
00:36:35,092 --> 00:36:38,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you chose to stay in the domain where things out of your control can happen.

591
00:36:38,914 --> 00:36:39,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Good or bad, doesn't matter.

592
00:36:41,875 --> 00:36:47,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognizing that there is the enlightenment, there is my enlightenment, my possibility.

593
00:36:49,019 --> 00:36:53,541
[SPEAKER_00]: And how can somebody, without acknowledging that existence of possibilities,

594
00:36:55,050 --> 00:37:01,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Without reverting to some explanatory third-person wrong view, how can then he take that on as his practice?

595
00:37:01,273 --> 00:37:01,633
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't.

596
00:37:02,494 --> 00:37:05,275
[SPEAKER_00]: His practice is going to have a magical connection to the result.

597
00:37:06,036 --> 00:37:18,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm devotional, I'm this, I'm doing that, I respect tradition, I meditate three hours a day, I watch my nostrils, I watch the soles of my feet, and then enlightenment will happen to me on account of these observances and duties that I do.

598
00:37:20,263 --> 00:37:20,403
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

599
00:37:20,783 --> 00:37:21,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Enlightenment cannot happen.

600
00:37:21,924 --> 00:37:22,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only develop it.

601
00:37:22,884 --> 00:37:24,505
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to develop it, you need to know what it is.

602
00:37:25,705 --> 00:37:31,608
[SPEAKER_00]: And how can you know what it is, when you're even denying the basic possibility of it being, in the first place?

603
00:37:34,010 --> 00:37:36,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, mundane view is very important.

604
00:37:38,792 --> 00:37:45,896
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's not to be confused... A lot of people would say that, like, it's not possible for me, in this lifetime.

605
00:37:45,916 --> 00:37:50,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's when you realize, like, unless you feel that it's your duty to become a sotāpanna, you will never do it.

606
00:37:51,699 --> 00:37:58,921
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a hard thing to do, and the only way to do it is through full acceptance of responsibility for not doing it, which would be more unpleasant than trying to do it.

607
00:37:59,581 --> 00:38:00,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you do it.

608
00:38:02,701 --> 00:38:06,062
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's your duty to become... to be free from suffering?

609
00:38:06,102 --> 00:38:06,282
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

610
00:38:06,782 --> 00:38:07,102
[SPEAKER_04]: Aha.

611
00:38:07,442 --> 00:38:08,903
[SPEAKER_04]: Not duty, responsibility.

612
00:38:08,923 --> 00:38:09,943
[SPEAKER_04]: Your responsibility, yeah.

613
00:38:10,483 --> 00:38:12,684
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it's your respons... your highest responsibility.

614
00:38:12,764 --> 00:38:14,904
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not free from suffering, it's on you.

615
00:38:15,624 --> 00:38:17,465
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're free from suffering, it's also on you.

616
00:38:17,885 --> 00:38:18,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have a choice.

617
00:38:20,612 --> 00:38:21,272
[SPEAKER_04]: No complaints.

618
00:38:21,733 --> 00:38:22,973
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

619
00:38:24,314 --> 00:38:25,655
[SPEAKER_04]: Layman.

620
00:38:25,675 --> 00:38:26,055
Yes.

621
00:38:28,957 --> 00:38:30,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's good that you said this.

622
00:38:30,658 --> 00:38:30,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

623
00:38:31,438 --> 00:38:32,459
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your responsibility.

624
00:38:32,499 --> 00:38:37,281
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stay in the realm of Mara, it's your responsibility, whatever happened to you.

625
00:38:38,742 --> 00:38:40,723
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, whatever kind of suffering.

626
00:38:44,485 --> 00:38:46,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'd be surprised how much people don't want to hear that.

627
00:38:48,047 --> 00:38:49,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, no, you can stay a layman.

628
00:38:50,258 --> 00:38:53,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever happens to you, literally, it's your fault.

629
00:38:53,440 --> 00:38:55,461
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to blame somebody, you can only blame yourself.

630
00:38:56,322 --> 00:38:58,964
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to blame somebody for enlightenment, you can only blame yourself.

631
00:39:00,645 --> 00:39:04,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And many people don't even want to admit that, because it's too unpleasant.

632
00:39:05,928 --> 00:39:10,751
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly... there are... there is my action, there are results of my action.

633
00:39:11,232 --> 00:39:17,295
[SPEAKER_00]: I... oh, my responsibility, my results, to the extent of infinity.

634
00:39:17,396 --> 00:39:17,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Who knows?

635
00:39:18,504 --> 00:39:20,025
[SPEAKER_00]: That domain, it just goes.

636
00:39:20,305 --> 00:39:20,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Who knows?

637
00:39:20,725 --> 00:39:27,748
[SPEAKER_00]: I have no idea which means it's not worth the risk then, isn't it?

638
00:39:28,789 --> 00:39:35,852
[SPEAKER_04]: Some people say that I cannot become arahant in this lifetime because of my karma.

639
00:39:36,613 --> 00:39:37,413
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

640
00:39:38,654 --> 00:39:38,874
[SPEAKER_00]: I know.

641
00:39:39,134 --> 00:39:42,836
[SPEAKER_00]: People say that because they talk about karma factually.

642
00:39:44,296 --> 00:39:45,237
[SPEAKER_00]: That would only work

643
00:39:46,438 --> 00:39:49,840
[SPEAKER_00]: If you genuinely develop your mind to the extent of seeing your past actions.

644
00:39:50,500 --> 00:39:57,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I ask you, if I come and slap you in the face, because yesterday you came and you stole my, I don't know, my property.

645
00:39:58,244 --> 00:40:00,145
[SPEAKER_00]: You, you say, okay, well, I deserved it.

646
00:40:00,986 --> 00:40:01,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

647
00:40:01,146 --> 00:40:02,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have a memory of it.

648
00:40:04,307 --> 00:40:09,130
[SPEAKER_00]: If you've done something 10 years ago, and somebody reminds you of it, you still say, okay, yeah, I've done that.

649
00:40:10,493 --> 00:40:16,797
[SPEAKER_00]: But now people try to sort of use kamma as an excuse for not doing anything, not having memory of it as well.

650
00:40:17,338 --> 00:40:25,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't remember what I've done, but I choose that lack of memory to be my excuse for not doing something now that I'm fully aware of I can do.

651
00:40:26,864 --> 00:40:32,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm saying it wouldn't work even if you were to have a memory of it, let alone when you don't have a memory of it.

652
00:40:33,108 --> 00:40:35,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if that's your kamma from before,

653
00:40:36,543 --> 00:40:38,084
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same karma from yesterday.

654
00:40:38,124 --> 00:40:40,185
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, what you've done yesterday is your karma.

655
00:40:40,205 --> 00:40:41,706
[SPEAKER_00]: What you've done ten years ago is your karma.

656
00:40:42,227 --> 00:40:43,207
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why stop then?

657
00:40:44,328 --> 00:40:50,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, maybe there are things that I've done ten lifetimes ago, and they will be as much as mine, as what I've done yesterday is mine.

658
00:40:51,493 --> 00:40:52,293
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the point.

659
00:40:52,394 --> 00:40:53,794
[SPEAKER_00]: They will be.

660
00:40:53,975 --> 00:40:55,055
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't be anyone else's.

661
00:40:56,176 --> 00:40:59,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But you choose to draw this invisible line, where your memory is.

662
00:40:59,658 --> 00:41:00,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't remember.

663
00:41:00,379 --> 00:41:00,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Not my problem.

664
00:41:01,840 --> 00:41:01,980
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

665
00:41:02,240 --> 00:41:03,201
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't actually go the other way.

666
00:41:03,321 --> 00:41:04,001
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember.

667
00:41:04,061 --> 00:41:05,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's a big problem.

668
00:41:06,467 --> 00:41:12,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, things I don't remember in this life, that I can, you know, end up paying price or somebody reminds me of, are a problem.

669
00:41:12,911 --> 00:41:15,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine things that you don't remember from previous lives.

670
00:41:16,793 --> 00:41:17,913
[SPEAKER_00]: How much of a problem they will be.

671
00:41:20,815 --> 00:41:25,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of using the lack of memory for kind of not feeling responsible, it should be the other way.

672
00:41:26,298 --> 00:41:30,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Lack of memory should make you responsible for everything, even if you have no memory of what it is.

673
00:41:32,021 --> 00:41:34,603
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's very unpleasant for the mind that wants to deny it.

674
00:41:35,458 --> 00:41:37,179
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not giving up the view of denying it.

675
00:41:40,682 --> 00:41:42,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you would feel responsible for something you've done.

676
00:41:42,924 --> 00:41:49,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, today I do this because ten years ago I started, like, I don't know, developing certain habit.

677
00:41:49,750 --> 00:41:53,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't deny responsibility for it, because you still have a memory of it.

678
00:41:56,015 --> 00:42:01,759
[SPEAKER_00]: But you deny responsibility because, yeah, maybe you can't practice properly because of your previous karma.

679
00:42:02,340 --> 00:42:03,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Your previous karma.

680
00:42:04,543 --> 00:42:05,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Your previous action.

681
00:42:06,204 --> 00:42:10,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you yourself are responsible for you yourself not being able to practice today.

682
00:42:12,367 --> 00:42:16,029
[SPEAKER_00]: That responsibility, whether it's from 10 years ago, 10 lifetimes ago, doesn't matter.

683
00:42:16,149 --> 00:42:18,270
[SPEAKER_00]: It's yours, as much as always will be.

684
00:42:19,130 --> 00:42:25,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can't become an arahant because of my previous karma means I can't become an arahant because I can't become an arahant.

685
00:42:26,914 --> 00:42:27,995
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reason in itself.

686
00:42:31,977 --> 00:42:33,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is, yeah, that is the reason.

687
00:42:34,794 --> 00:42:40,840
[SPEAKER_00]: But by kind of creating the loop of all khandhā memory, you kind of obscure your responsibility for it.

688
00:42:42,441 --> 00:42:47,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and I think it's probably also connected with what you said before, with these observances.

689
00:42:48,046 --> 00:42:48,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

690
00:42:48,507 --> 00:42:52,951
[SPEAKER_01]: The person is following some kind of observances, some kind of practice without results.

691
00:42:53,692 --> 00:42:53,912
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

692
00:42:54,403 --> 00:42:59,444
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, this kind of results, so then, he would end up with this kind of excuse, you know.

693
00:42:59,844 --> 00:43:00,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

694
00:43:00,104 --> 00:43:03,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he was maybe meditating, he's been meditating 20, 30 years.

695
00:43:03,365 --> 00:43:08,226
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they would end up, they would end up with an excuse that reveals, relieves them of responsibility.

696
00:43:08,246 --> 00:43:08,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

697
00:43:08,746 --> 00:43:11,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the excuse of, wait a minute, maybe I've been doing it wrong.

698
00:43:11,527 --> 00:43:11,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

699
00:43:12,587 --> 00:43:12,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no.

700
00:43:13,327 --> 00:43:17,488
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't have been doing it wrong, because that would mean my efforts were wasted, but that's too unpleasant to admit.

701
00:43:18,248 --> 00:43:20,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead, I'll just dismiss it.

702
00:43:20,109 --> 00:43:20,309
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

703
00:43:20,509 --> 00:43:21,949
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just say, well, no, I'm doing everything

704
00:43:23,173 --> 00:43:25,135
[SPEAKER_00]: But I can't get to it because of something in the past.

705
00:43:25,416 --> 00:43:26,397
[SPEAKER_00]: They have no memory of it.

706
00:43:26,877 --> 00:43:30,121
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something have no memory of it, means it doesn't exist for you.

707
00:43:30,702 --> 00:43:39,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, that which doesn't exist for you, you have no knowledge of, no memory, no perception of it, you use as a presently perceived reason for your failure.

708
00:43:42,575 --> 00:43:47,421
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you see how wrong that is, on a deeply rooted self-deceptive act of self-deception.

709
00:43:47,441 --> 00:43:55,532
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really interesting what you are saying, because just recently I heard about one well-known teacher who would openly admit his kutujana,

710
00:43:56,523 --> 00:44:01,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And he can't, in this life, because of this previous kamma, achieve anything.

711
00:44:01,505 --> 00:44:01,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

712
00:44:01,885 --> 00:44:06,387
[SPEAKER_01]: But he is a well-known teacher and he can teach others, still, to practice.

713
00:44:06,887 --> 00:44:07,647
[SPEAKER_01]: So, here I am...

714
00:44:07,667 --> 00:44:13,229
[SPEAKER_01]: They can, by his advices, because they are not bothered by that bad kamma he has.

715
00:44:13,249 --> 00:44:14,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure, sure.

716
00:44:14,050 --> 00:44:20,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Because his advice is obviously on the level of mechanical instruction, that anybody can do or not.

717
00:44:20,972 --> 00:44:25,154
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... if you have a view that that's what dhamma practice is, then, yeah, anybody can be the teacher.

718
00:44:26,149 --> 00:44:27,450
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not what a dhamma is, isn't it?

719
00:44:27,570 --> 00:44:34,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as we explained, the mundane view requires you to take on responsibility, which is not... cannot be done mechanically.

720
00:44:35,576 --> 00:44:41,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can do... go through the same motions as I do, but your attitude is attitude of not accepting responsibility.

721
00:44:43,382 --> 00:44:48,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can give you verbatim instruction, it won't work, because your attitude doesn't work, which means there's no dhamma there.

722
00:44:49,346 --> 00:44:52,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you going through these motions is going through the literal motions.

723
00:44:53,425 --> 00:44:54,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Without a dhamma.

724
00:44:54,825 --> 00:44:56,426
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Sīla Bhāgavatam Master means.

725
00:44:56,446 --> 00:44:58,426
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean rituals and rites and whatnot.

726
00:44:58,666 --> 00:45:02,046
[SPEAKER_00]: So, things that are obviously ridiculous, when it comes to practice.

727
00:45:02,467 --> 00:45:05,287
[SPEAKER_00]: It means virtue, sīla, and duty.

728
00:45:05,347 --> 00:45:06,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of duty.

729
00:45:06,807 --> 00:45:10,228
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's my duty to sit cross-legged and meditate as a monk.

730
00:45:11,148 --> 00:45:12,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but why you do it?

731
00:45:12,708 --> 00:45:14,048
[SPEAKER_00]: To what extent does it help you?

732
00:45:14,269 --> 00:45:15,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you understand why you're doing it?

733
00:45:16,069 --> 00:45:16,229
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

734
00:45:16,789 --> 00:45:17,569
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just doing it.

735
00:45:18,169 --> 00:45:19,469
[SPEAKER_00]: It's my duty to do this.

736
00:45:19,869 --> 00:45:21,430
[SPEAKER_00]: And my virtue is also my duty.

737
00:45:21,950 --> 00:45:22,690
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a factor.

738
00:45:23,733 --> 00:45:31,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you abandon that fetter without abandoning your virtue, without abandoning your efforts?

739
00:45:32,099 --> 00:45:33,620
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what differentiates it.

740
00:45:34,080 --> 00:45:38,763
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if it's a duty, means you bypass responsibility at least a little bit.

741
00:45:39,304 --> 00:45:40,144
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, I'm a judge.

742
00:45:40,525 --> 00:45:42,646
[SPEAKER_00]: It's my duty to pass a judgment onto you.

743
00:45:43,947 --> 00:45:46,189
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I'm not responsible for sending you to jail.

744
00:45:47,049 --> 00:45:50,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, well, you chose to be the judge, which is the one who sends people to jail.

745
00:45:51,914 --> 00:45:56,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you introduce the proxy to cover up the fact that you are the one condemning people to die or not.

746
00:45:58,037 --> 00:45:58,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but it's my duty.

747
00:45:59,458 --> 00:45:59,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it?

748
00:45:59,818 --> 00:46:00,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because I need a job.

749
00:46:01,199 --> 00:46:02,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you need a job?

750
00:46:02,299 --> 00:46:03,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I have a family.

751
00:46:03,400 --> 00:46:04,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you have a family?

752
00:46:04,180 --> 00:46:05,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you had sex with your wife.

753
00:46:06,322 --> 00:46:07,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Was that also your duty?

754
00:46:08,403 --> 00:46:08,803
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't.

755
00:46:09,423 --> 00:46:13,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've done things you wanted to do and now you're refusing to accept responsibility for it.

756
00:46:14,306 --> 00:46:15,006
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to work.

757
00:46:15,106 --> 00:46:16,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, because you have six children.

758
00:46:17,895 --> 00:46:21,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Generally, it would be your duty if somebody forced those children on you and you had to look out.

759
00:46:21,717 --> 00:46:23,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Even then, it wouldn't be your duty.

760
00:46:23,518 --> 00:46:27,460
[SPEAKER_00]: You would be responsible or rather you would be free to accept it or not.

761
00:46:28,961 --> 00:46:30,161
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that scary bit.

762
00:46:30,682 --> 00:46:38,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, no matter which way your life went, what you invested in it, one morning you can wake up and decide to just... and leave all.

763
00:46:39,266 --> 00:46:39,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

764
00:46:39,886 --> 00:46:40,807
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your responsibility.

765
00:46:41,007 --> 00:46:44,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And having that, that edge present all the time, freaks people out.

766
00:46:46,130 --> 00:46:50,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind is undeveloped, the mind is unrestrained, the mind has no basis of virtue and self-control.

767
00:46:51,612 --> 00:46:56,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just a sight of the edge makes the mind run in terror.

768
00:46:56,794 --> 00:47:02,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's probably the most, like, scary, scary thing to accept your responsibility.

769
00:47:02,415 --> 00:47:04,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the most scary thing for monks when they are dead.

770
00:47:04,656 --> 00:47:09,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, like, my duty, my duty, my virtue, I must do this, vinaya, vinaya, and get extremely neurotic.

771
00:47:10,398 --> 00:47:11,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, it's not even vinaya anymore.

772
00:47:11,899 --> 00:47:15,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just some obscure, ridiculous interpretation upon interpretation.

773
00:47:15,700 --> 00:47:16,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the rule is clear.

774
00:47:16,761 --> 00:47:17,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Context is clear.

775
00:47:17,601 --> 00:47:18,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is clear.

776
00:47:18,702 --> 00:47:23,424
[SPEAKER_00]: But a person can't sleep for five days just because mind is like, oh, what if, what if, what if.

777
00:47:25,184 --> 00:47:28,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Because through avoiding responsibility, you actually make things worse for yourself.

778
00:47:28,986 --> 00:47:30,447
[SPEAKER_00]: They can overwhelm you.

779
00:47:30,507 --> 00:47:33,488
[SPEAKER_00]: And whenever, whenever circumstances present themselves in their way.

780
00:47:34,456 --> 00:47:38,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, it's going to be much more unpleasant to accept responsibility for your virtue.

781
00:47:38,146 --> 00:47:39,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, I am choosing to do this.

782
00:47:40,192 --> 00:47:42,839
[SPEAKER_00]: I am choosing... it will then enable you to draw a line as well.

783
00:47:44,446 --> 00:47:44,707
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

784
00:47:44,767 --> 00:47:49,950
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not think that, because I know exactly what's right.

785
00:47:50,230 --> 00:47:51,751
[SPEAKER_00]: And even those people know exactly what's right.

786
00:47:52,271 --> 00:48:03,358
[SPEAKER_00]: But because they're projected in this third-person view of duty, something that must be done, that's nothing to do with me, then they also keep needing the third person's confirmation and affirmation of what they're doing to be right.

787
00:48:03,699 --> 00:48:08,822
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the doubt can only be settled by going around and asking people, and so they tell you what you want to hear,

788
00:48:09,671 --> 00:48:23,158
[SPEAKER_00]: By reading, reading, reinterpreting, interpreting, interpreting, until you get what you want to hear, by having everybody else does the same rites and rituals you do, because if they don't, it will question the validity of the stuff you do, everything is out there, nothing is in me, nothing is my responsibility.

789
00:48:23,458 --> 00:48:28,621
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why those people would generally be upset, existentially threatened, if you wear your sabong differently.

790
00:48:30,002 --> 00:48:39,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, here we wear flip-flops, he doesn't wear flip-flops, mind already panic, because there is there is a different way about this, which means it's not a duty then, the way I took it to be.

791
00:48:40,391 --> 00:48:43,854
[SPEAKER_00]: No, in this tradition, we wear our robe like this, and we put our cups down like this.

792
00:48:45,115 --> 00:48:47,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, I'm part of this tradition, and I put my cup like this.

793
00:48:48,458 --> 00:48:48,838
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

794
00:48:49,118 --> 00:48:49,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Meltdown.

795
00:48:50,779 --> 00:48:52,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Because their practice is out there.

796
00:48:53,021 --> 00:48:56,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this is what must be done, which means this is what must be done by everyone.

797
00:48:56,824 --> 00:49:00,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they don't do it, then I realize I'm responsible for keep assuming that this is what must be done.

798
00:49:01,428 --> 00:49:10,035
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it brings you back onto that personal responsibility that you wanted to avoid in the first place, which is why you latched onto that factor of duty and virtue.

799
00:49:12,092 --> 00:49:15,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no dhamma without sīla, but dhamma is not a sīla.

800
00:49:15,675 --> 00:49:16,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

801
00:49:17,076 --> 00:49:20,379
