802
00:49:20,399 --> 00:49:23,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I understand, it's basically about this attitude.

803
00:49:24,002 --> 00:49:28,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you change that attitude, you remove just paramāsa, but the sīla remains the same, basically.

804
00:49:28,606 --> 00:49:29,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

805
00:49:29,307 --> 00:49:34,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, sotāpanna doesn't abandon his sīla to become a sotāpanna, as in, he starts doing the opposite.

806
00:49:34,331 --> 00:49:35,853
[SPEAKER_01]: So, outwardly, you maybe don't...

807
00:49:36,333 --> 00:49:38,494
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see any difference in behavior of that person.

808
00:49:38,574 --> 00:49:39,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely, yeah.

809
00:49:39,655 --> 00:49:43,817
[SPEAKER_00]: But that person who is fully projected in that external third-person view, they can't see that.

810
00:49:44,257 --> 00:49:48,179
[SPEAKER_00]: For them, saying dhamma is not sīla means, so I must not keep sīla then.

811
00:49:48,819 --> 00:49:49,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

812
00:49:50,260 --> 00:50:04,047
[SPEAKER_00]: If you say that sīla, bhāta-paramāsa, is a factor of you assuming this is my duty and virtue by doing so, avoid personal responsibility for it, for them that means, okay, so I should disrobe and go and, you know, find a wife and so on.

813
00:50:04,387 --> 00:50:04,567
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

814
00:50:05,068 --> 00:50:05,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean that at all.

815
00:50:06,902 --> 00:50:10,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Sīla remains unchanged, but your attitude is now changed.

816
00:50:11,124 --> 00:50:16,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not you doing it anymore on account of the external assumed authority or whatever.

817
00:50:16,567 --> 00:50:25,751
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it because you see it exactly as a necessary basis for discerning the nature of phenomena of your mind, feelings and intentions, which you now understood is the way out of suffering.

818
00:50:28,612 --> 00:50:36,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless that responsibility is taken on for doing it in the first place, even the wrong things you're doing, even the fetters you're holding, you can't uproot them.

819
00:50:38,172 --> 00:50:43,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you deny that is a fetter, you cannot undo that fetter, because you're not seeing it.

820
00:50:45,015 --> 00:50:48,417
[SPEAKER_04]: What would be another translation of that sīla pāṭṭhā?

821
00:50:48,637 --> 00:50:50,558
[SPEAKER_00]: I think virtue and duty is a good translation.

822
00:50:50,898 --> 00:50:53,259
[SPEAKER_04]: The non-responsibility in virtue.

823
00:50:54,260 --> 00:50:55,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, sure, sure, sure.

824
00:50:55,520 --> 00:50:57,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Forecasting of your responsibility.

825
00:50:57,541 --> 00:50:58,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, yeah.

826
00:50:58,382 --> 00:51:02,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, virtue and duty as a basis of you avoiding responsibility for virtue and duty.

827
00:51:04,645 --> 00:51:06,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But sīla can be also like a behavior.

828
00:51:07,838 --> 00:51:10,160
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, that's the one meaning of behavior, sīla.

829
00:51:10,200 --> 00:51:11,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, behavior, sure.

830
00:51:11,641 --> 00:51:13,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can have also vichā, sīla.

831
00:51:13,882 --> 00:51:14,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

832
00:51:15,383 --> 00:51:24,390
