940
00:58:12,993 --> 00:58:13,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

941
00:58:14,154 --> 00:58:17,195
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it cannot be independent atta.

942
00:58:17,295 --> 00:58:18,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not, but it's not like...

943
00:58:22,297 --> 00:58:34,063
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why what you just said is not accurate is because even when you have a thought of what externally conditions your experience, that cannot be that external because of which your experience is conditioned.

944
00:58:36,058 --> 00:58:40,462
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you experience that which is beyond your experience, unless you experience it?

945
00:58:40,722 --> 00:58:40,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

946
00:58:41,202 --> 00:58:47,168
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're experiencing it, it cannot be external to your experience, because it's your experience.

947
00:58:47,248 --> 00:58:50,510
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean external to the sense of me.

948
00:58:50,530 --> 00:58:51,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

949
00:58:51,431 --> 00:58:54,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Any notion of external, it's internal for you already.

950
00:58:57,597 --> 00:58:59,178
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you are put out of other.

951
00:59:00,325 --> 00:59:02,832
[SPEAKER_00]: But not leaving, not giving it any way out.

952
00:59:03,253 --> 00:59:08,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are always actually in... Well, you have no choice, of course, but your view doesn't think that.

953
00:59:10,385 --> 00:59:17,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any form of allowing, any form of external, it's allowing the basis for the for the ātavāda to be there.

954
00:59:17,430 --> 00:59:28,698
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, even when you say, my ātavāda is conditioned by external, you already assumed external, independent of your experience, and that's the basis for your very ātavāda you're trying to undo.

955
00:59:28,919 --> 00:59:29,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, I got it.

956
00:59:29,759 --> 00:59:33,382
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, can you always, like, try to catch this this kind of point?

957
00:59:34,007 --> 00:59:35,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the whole point.

958
00:59:35,548 --> 00:59:38,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep bringing the point back to your mind, and then not losing it.

959
00:59:39,331 --> 00:59:47,477
[SPEAKER_00]: The point that, no matter what sense of self you yourself might be, lofty, far, internal, external, it's all within the aggregates.

960
00:59:47,537 --> 00:59:52,661
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you think it's all within, it's not external, that thought of that external is already within it.

961
00:59:53,462 --> 00:59:58,406
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop providing basis for it, because it needs constant nutriment.

962
00:59:58,886 --> 01:00:03,810
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove the basis, then the nature of it will become apparent after a period of time.

963
01:00:04,892 --> 01:00:09,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just repeat, basically, the same kind of process to, like, to catch this... Yeah.

964
01:00:09,743 --> 01:00:14,754
[SPEAKER_00]: What's gonna happen is, you start repeating it, it's gonna all make sense in the beginning, but then you start doubting it.

965
01:00:15,250 --> 01:00:18,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you will have to find a way of getting back to it through that doubt.

966
01:00:19,032 --> 01:00:22,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And keep... because each time you start doubting, it means you're reaching to a new level of it.

967
01:00:22,674 --> 01:00:22,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

968
01:00:23,394 --> 01:00:26,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, a new idea, you're so inspired, everything makes sense.

969
01:00:26,256 --> 01:00:26,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

970
01:00:26,536 --> 01:00:30,078
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been doing it, then that inspiration runs away, and then you just start doubting it.

971
01:00:30,098 --> 01:00:30,938
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't see it.

972
01:00:30,958 --> 01:00:32,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you have to keep pushing forward.

973
01:00:32,799 --> 01:00:33,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

974
01:00:33,340 --> 01:00:35,581
[SPEAKER_00]: But the whole point is, yeah, develop the theme,

975
01:00:36,498 --> 01:00:41,141
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't provide the basis for any notion of external in regard to your experience of the world.

976
01:00:41,201 --> 01:00:41,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

977
01:00:42,362 --> 01:00:43,623
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like there is no external.

978
01:00:43,923 --> 01:00:49,247
[SPEAKER_00]: There is external, which is experienced by you, which means that external is fundamentally internal.

979
01:00:50,007 --> 01:00:50,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Very good.

980
01:00:50,728 --> 01:01:00,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, I realized one thing that you cannot make any kind, like a progress, if you try to catch these pañca khandhas, these five aggregates, as that external.

981
01:01:01,210 --> 01:01:06,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that you keep assuming that you could, means you keep allowing possibility of external.

982
01:01:07,173 --> 01:01:08,614
[SPEAKER_00]: And that in itself is already wrong.

983
01:01:09,094 --> 01:01:09,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

984
01:01:09,675 --> 01:01:16,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, I was like watching, trying to watch these pañcakandhas, you know, like that matter and other things that they are, maybe impermanent, whatever.

985
01:01:16,959 --> 01:01:24,383
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't move further, because you are always like watching something... Well, your watching of pañcakandha is within pañcakandha.

986
01:01:24,423 --> 01:01:24,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

987
01:01:26,063 --> 01:01:31,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, assuming, like, assuming any form of out of it is the basis for rātavāda.

988
01:01:31,425 --> 01:01:32,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't...

989
01:01:32,465 --> 01:01:43,449
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't, like, I will stop rātavāda, but what I can do is stop maintaining this obvious contradiction of assuming external through these little unquestioned attitudes and habits and thoughts.

990
01:01:43,529 --> 01:01:48,751
[SPEAKER_00]: You undo that to the sufficient, like, extent necessary, there will be no room for rātavāda.

991
01:01:49,131 --> 01:01:50,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you realize, yeah, there is a self.

992
01:01:51,828 --> 01:01:53,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing when people say there's no self.

993
01:01:53,651 --> 01:01:54,152
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no self.

994
01:01:54,413 --> 01:01:54,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

995
01:01:55,114 --> 01:01:55,775
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there is self.

996
01:01:56,416 --> 01:01:56,997
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not yours.

997
01:01:57,985 --> 01:01:59,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But that thing which is self is there.

998
01:01:59,846 --> 01:02:00,526
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that thing?

999
01:02:00,766 --> 01:02:01,647
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pañca khandhā.

1000
01:02:02,227 --> 01:02:03,028
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

1001
01:02:03,608 --> 01:02:07,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But people don't even arrive at it, because their whole practice... there is no self.

1002
01:02:07,710 --> 01:02:11,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is the external view of my aggregates already, modern science, what not.

1003
01:02:11,592 --> 01:02:15,594
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I just extend it even further with another layer of wrong view, saying there is no self.

1004
01:02:15,754 --> 01:02:17,095
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

1005
01:02:17,155 --> 01:02:19,156
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just deny your denial even further.

1006
01:02:19,756 --> 01:02:23,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, all of it is assumed external to you, which means you're through and through atavāda.

1007
01:02:24,063 --> 01:02:31,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you need to admit it, that there is that sense of self, and then stop putting it external to the experience as a whole, but see it within it.

1008
01:02:32,085 --> 01:02:32,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Change the order.

1009
01:02:32,905 --> 01:02:34,526
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you need to do.

1010
01:02:34,546 --> 01:02:36,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, discover new things.

1011
01:02:36,066 --> 01:02:41,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep, eh... sorry, cease perverting them, perverting the order.

1012
01:02:41,748 --> 01:02:42,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Cease messing them up.

1013
01:02:43,488 --> 01:02:49,750
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a person who understood, would understand the Buddha for the first time, would say, oh, it's like putting the second first and the first second.

1014
01:02:50,994 --> 01:02:52,155
[SPEAKER_00]: The Tathāgata cleared it out.

1015
01:02:52,215 --> 01:02:54,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like what was upside down, now it's proper.

1016
01:02:54,297 --> 01:02:54,757
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

1017
01:02:55,338 --> 01:02:56,619
[SPEAKER_00]: You can take it in a poetic sense.

1018
01:02:56,639 --> 01:02:59,261
[SPEAKER_00]: You can take it in a very literal sense, which is exactly what it is.

1019
01:02:59,441 --> 01:03:00,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, oh, yeah.

1020
01:03:00,722 --> 01:03:01,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of self.

1021
01:03:02,123 --> 01:03:04,766
[SPEAKER_00]: The world, it's actually sense within the world.

1022
01:03:04,906 --> 01:03:06,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable to be outside.

1023
01:03:06,347 --> 01:03:07,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, anybody can... Oh, yeah, I get that.

1024
01:03:08,028 --> 01:03:08,509
[SPEAKER_00]: I get that.

1025
01:03:08,969 --> 01:03:09,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, great.

1026
01:03:09,850 --> 01:03:11,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, develop that theme day and night.

1027
01:03:12,752 --> 01:03:13,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't abandon it.

1028
01:03:14,122 --> 01:03:15,843
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what you're going through in your life.

1029
01:03:16,183 --> 01:03:22,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't abandon the theme that your sense of self can never be external in regard to your thought of external as well.

1030
01:03:22,668 --> 01:03:23,048
[SPEAKER_00]: I see.

1031
01:03:23,769 --> 01:03:31,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you will be sort of literally sucking out the oxygen that's necessary for that sense of self to keep enduring in that external manner.

1032
01:03:32,895 --> 01:03:33,855
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no basis for it.

1033
01:03:33,895 --> 01:03:35,477
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be maintained.

1034
01:03:39,153 --> 01:03:45,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, there's nothing mystical about it, but there is nothing scientific about it either, because science is already the wrong view.

1035
01:03:46,799 --> 01:03:48,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to have instruction.

1036
01:03:48,240 --> 01:03:49,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, this is what I need to look for.

1037
01:03:50,382 --> 01:04:01,470
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you need to start discerning it in your own experience, because if I tell you this, and now you go as a... and make a mantra out of it, and just mechanically repeat things that I told you, you're not going to do anything.

1038
01:04:02,933 --> 01:04:13,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's not incidental that all these people, they misquote Ajahn Chah, are also people who praise Buddha, Buddha meditation, where you're just repeating Buddha, rhythmically, and that results in samādhi.

1039
01:04:14,661 --> 01:04:14,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1040
01:04:16,122 --> 01:04:17,383
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it's quite... Good luck with that samādhi.

1041
01:04:17,403 --> 01:04:18,204
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite amazing.

1042
01:04:18,284 --> 01:04:26,990
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't figure out how you came to these conclusions by yourself, you know, just reading suttas, because I'm also reading them, but I can't see these things.

1043
01:04:27,030 --> 01:04:30,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I've never been avoiding my responsibility from the beginning.

1044
01:04:32,988 --> 01:04:40,415
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where what you read in the suttas start to apply much more accurately.

1045
01:04:40,675 --> 01:04:43,397
[SPEAKER_00]: It's less pleasant, but that's not optional.

1046
01:04:45,719 --> 01:04:48,221
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no other way like you can do by avoiding this.

1047
01:04:48,802 --> 01:04:53,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You can suffer to different accounts on account of it, but it's the same thing that needs to be done.

1048
01:04:55,308 --> 01:04:57,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And people who suffer on different accounts, people who like, you know,

1049
01:05:00,485 --> 01:05:08,507
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have been developed, sort of, like, have proper samādhi, proper virtue, then you suffer less, on account of the same thing.

1050
01:05:08,607 --> 01:05:16,049
[SPEAKER_00]: If your starting point, even as a layman, is right there, without sufficient proper samādhi, or sufficient proper virtue, you will suffer much more.

1051
01:05:16,970 --> 01:05:19,190
[SPEAKER_00]: But nevertheless, either way, the point is to arrive at it.

1052
01:05:20,931 --> 01:05:22,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you can read all you want.

1053
01:05:22,931 --> 01:05:28,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for goodness sake, I mean, people get into nirnaveda and completely misquote it and misrepresent it.

1054
01:05:29,617 --> 01:05:31,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Not out of malice, out of complete misunderstanding.

1055
01:05:31,779 --> 01:05:32,859
[SPEAKER_00]: No idea what he's talking about.

1056
01:05:33,360 --> 01:05:35,541
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a dedicated life to the teaching in the Veda.

1057
01:05:36,782 --> 01:05:41,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you hear the views, and it's quite shocking how they're able to maintain such contradiction.

1058
01:05:41,546 --> 01:05:46,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he could not have been more blunt and more clear in many other ways.

1059
01:05:46,189 --> 01:05:54,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that won't make any difference if there is not even a mundane acceptance of responsibility and transparency in regard to your own intentions and actions.

1060
01:05:56,060 --> 01:05:58,361
[SPEAKER_00]: And that doesn't mean just, yes, I'm responsible.

1061
01:05:58,381 --> 01:06:00,801
[SPEAKER_00]: That means that, no, I must feel this.

1062
01:06:00,861 --> 01:06:06,303
[SPEAKER_00]: I must feel this existential anxiety on account of responsibility for things that I don't even have a memory of.

1063
01:06:07,163 --> 01:06:10,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's a very unpleasant state to begin with.

1064
01:06:10,604 --> 01:06:14,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you start accepting it, then you realize practice of dhamma, at first, is not optional.

1065
01:06:15,626 --> 01:06:20,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Secondly, it's not up to anyone else or any external pattern, form, method, institution.

1066
01:06:21,107 --> 01:06:23,708
[SPEAKER_00]: It's upon me being free from craving or not free from craving.

1067
01:06:24,778 --> 01:06:25,098
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

1068
01:06:25,378 --> 01:06:28,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also how you realize that practice of dhamma doesn't manage your suffering.

1069
01:06:29,000 --> 01:06:30,021
[SPEAKER_00]: It uproots it altogether.

1070
01:06:30,441 --> 01:06:32,802
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't suffer in the first place, thus there's nothing to manage.

1071
01:06:32,922 --> 01:06:36,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you're free from any burden, any duty, anything you must do.

1072
01:06:37,525 --> 01:06:37,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

1073
01:06:38,645 --> 01:06:39,766
[SPEAKER_00]: No reason to do anything anymore.

1074
01:06:40,847 --> 01:06:48,611
[SPEAKER_00]: But you won't arrive to that through blindly adhering to the dependence on needing something to do, which prevents you from admitting that you're responsible for that.

1075
01:06:49,831 --> 01:06:51,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't hold to fixed views.

1076
01:06:51,492 --> 01:06:52,353
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your fixed view.

1077
01:06:53,550 --> 01:06:54,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You must not trust anything.

1078
01:06:54,930 --> 01:06:56,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you shouldn't trust even that statement.

1079
01:07:00,051 --> 01:07:03,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Does Sotāpanna have suffering?

1080
01:07:04,372 --> 01:07:05,893
[SPEAKER_00]: From Arahant's point of view, yes.

1081
01:07:06,013 --> 01:07:07,433
[SPEAKER_00]: From Bhutujana's point of view, no.

1082
01:07:10,854 --> 01:07:12,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing the way out of suffering means you can't suffer.

1083
01:07:14,294 --> 01:07:15,215
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Sotāpanna is.

1084
01:07:15,335 --> 01:07:16,415
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows the way out of suffering.

1085
01:07:20,331 --> 01:07:29,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if putujana doesn't accept, doesn't take that as a criteria, he will then think that he knows what sotāpanna is, to what extent, while still being a putujana.

1086
01:07:29,678 --> 01:07:31,019
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a contradiction in terms.

1087
01:07:31,840 --> 01:07:32,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1088
01:07:32,481 --> 01:07:35,323
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to know sotāpanna is to become one, as you already said.

1089
01:07:36,764 --> 01:07:41,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is that suffering which, from the point of Arahant, is in sotāpanna?

1090
01:07:42,308 --> 01:07:45,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Symptoms of the illness that are left while illness has been cured.

1091
01:07:46,932 --> 01:07:48,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nuisance.

1092
01:07:49,180 --> 01:07:49,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Nuisance.

1093
01:07:50,520 --> 01:07:50,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, nuisance.

1094
01:07:50,980 --> 01:07:51,560
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

1095
01:07:52,861 --> 01:07:53,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you're dying of cancer.

1096
01:07:54,421 --> 01:07:54,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1097
01:07:55,501 --> 01:07:59,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, your joints ache, your lymph nodes are swollen, everything hurts, everything is unpleasant.

1098
01:08:00,182 --> 01:08:03,463
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, you mentally suffer and you're dying of cancer.

1099
01:08:04,563 --> 01:08:06,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, I come and I cure your cancer.

1100
01:08:07,823 --> 01:08:08,783
[SPEAKER_00]: But your joints still hurt.

1101
01:08:09,584 --> 01:08:10,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You still don't have hair.

1102
01:08:10,544 --> 01:08:12,484
[SPEAKER_00]: You still... your lymph nodes are still swollen.

1103
01:08:12,704 --> 01:08:14,004
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know you're free from cancer.

1104
01:08:14,404 --> 01:08:15,525
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd be quite joyous, actually.

1105
01:08:15,905 --> 01:08:17,445
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't mind any of this discomfort.

1106
01:08:18,347 --> 01:08:26,509
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, from the point of view of somebody who is free from even those residual symptoms, yeah, you can say there is some sort of suffering.

1107
01:08:27,089 --> 01:08:30,930
[SPEAKER_00]: But not from the point of view of somebody who is still in sheer terror of dying of cancer.

1108
01:08:31,490 --> 01:08:33,631
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not even bothered by his symptoms.

1109
01:08:34,191 --> 01:08:36,551
[SPEAKER_00]: His only bother is the fact that he's dying of cancer.

1110
01:08:38,412 --> 01:08:41,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you approve that, then symptoms won't bother you even more.

1111
01:08:41,893 --> 01:08:42,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Symptoms will bother you even less.

1112
01:08:44,014 --> 01:08:46,755
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sotāpanna can turn away and sort of go back and whatever.

1113
01:08:47,736 --> 01:08:52,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Or lay people can be sotāpannas and so on.

1114
01:08:52,138 --> 01:08:55,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Still live the life and be attracted to sensuality and everything else.

1115
01:08:55,720 --> 01:09:00,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have uprooted the illness completely and you fully understand that.

1116
01:09:00,663 --> 01:09:02,224
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, what is happening to me?

1117
01:09:02,304 --> 01:09:03,984
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you know exactly what's happening.

1118
01:09:04,945 --> 01:09:08,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no amount of symptoms can reintroduce the illness.

1119
01:09:08,927 --> 01:09:10,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And that gives tremendous confidence.

1120
01:09:11,135 --> 01:09:13,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of which, you can become careless and complacent, because you don't care.

1121
01:09:14,497 --> 01:09:16,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimately, you know that you cannot become ill.

1122
01:09:17,899 --> 01:09:18,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what you do.

1123
01:09:19,259 --> 01:09:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: But he still doesn't have a full understanding of things.

1124
01:09:22,681 --> 01:09:30,085
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he has full understanding, but he has not dwelt enough in the theme of full understanding, not long enough.

1125
01:09:32,080 --> 01:09:35,061
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, I just gave you the how to uproot ātavāda.

1126
01:09:35,521 --> 01:09:38,203
[SPEAKER_00]: You must... you can't just think about the theme once or twice.

1127
01:09:38,723 --> 01:09:45,105
[SPEAKER_00]: You must keep discerning it and dwell in it for as long as it takes, until the handle of the axe wears out.

1128
01:09:45,246 --> 01:09:46,646
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know when it is.

1129
01:09:49,447 --> 01:09:54,289
[SPEAKER_00]: For Sauribhāna, it's even clearer than that, because he knows exactly what to think and what themes to develop.

1130
01:09:55,370 --> 01:09:56,110
[SPEAKER_00]: He just needs to do it.

1131
01:09:56,730 --> 01:10:00,532
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha keeps encouraging causing arising sense of urgency.

1132
01:10:03,543 --> 01:10:08,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for most putuginas, when they think sotāpanna, it's pretty much almost an arahant.

1133
01:10:08,591 --> 01:10:11,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, it's basically almost like what I am, a putugin.

1134
01:10:12,016 --> 01:10:13,819
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't see sotāpanna for what it is.

1135
01:10:13,859 --> 01:10:15,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, they're not a sotāpanna.

